        {
          "source_quote": "οὐδὲ τὸν υἱόν τις οἶδεν, εἰ μὴ ὁ πατὴρ καὶ οἷς ἂν βούληται ὁ υἱὸς ἀποκαλύψαι",
          "english_quote": "nor does anyone know the Son except the Father and those to whom the Son wishes to reveal him.",
          "issue": "The Greek leaves the object of ἀποκαλύψαι unexpressed. The English supplies 'him', naturally referring to the Father rather than reflexively to the Son. Revealing the Father is defensible from the surrounding discussion, including 18.10, but the placement of καὶ οἷς after the exception concerning knowledge of the Son also permits an interpretation involving revelation of the Son or his identity. The notes disclose other substantial ambiguities but not this supplied-object decision. The same decision recurs in the later quotation.",
          "suggested_revision": "Either leave the object unexpressed in both quotation occurrences, using 'those to whom the Son wishes to reveal', with a note explaining the possible objects, or retain 'him' with a source-quoted note identifying the Father as the chosen referent, explaining the contextual grounds, and preserving the alternative involving the Son or knowledge of him. Do not rearrange the quotation to match a familiar biblical form. Any revised wording requires fresh review; the ambiguity itself does not require blocking.",
